  "summary": "This two-page circular, dated 10 January 1977 and issued by Madhu Mehta as National Convenor of the Hindustani Movement, presents “Manav Nirman” (human-making) as a programme of ethical citizenship. It defines a human being through nine “mantras”: sharing happiness, relieving others’ suffering, resisting injustice and exploitation, practising fairness and courtesy, respecting all religions, overcoming prejudice, rejecting hypocrisy and pride, learning from mistakes, and seeking and living by truth. The circular argues that genuine national change cannot be achieved through slogans, propaganda, elections, or a change of government alone; India must overcome “poverty of the soul and spirit” alongside material poverty.",
    "extent_caveat": false,
    "summary_structured": {
      "key_points": [
        "The circular defines Manav Nirman through nine ethical commitments concerning compassion, justice, equality, truth, and self-correction.",
        "It links national progress to the humanisation of public and private conduct rather than to political slogans or institutional change alone.",
        "It calls for practical service on 30 January 1977, including feeding hungry people, spending time with children, helping implement at least one point of the government’s 25-point programme, and serving local communities.",
        "It urges opposition workers and political leaders to stop focusing obsessively on Prime Minister Indira Gandhi and instead provide concrete service to the public.",
        "It appeals for a joint prayer meeting involving Indira Gandhi and Jayaprakash Narayan at Rajghat on Gandhi Nirvan Din.",
        "The document frames political disagreement as compatible with shared national devotion, ethical responsibility, and a common commitment to India’s progress."
